  "query_preview": "..\n\nMild start to the week, followed by a cold and raw end to the work \nweek.\n\nAn upper-level ridge axis over the central Great Lakes region this \nafternoon will slide east, allowing a weak, moisture-starved cold \nfront/trough to cross Southeast Michigan Tuesday morning. Despite \nlow-level winds veering to the west-northwest Tuesday afternoon, a \ndry antecedent airmass (mean RH around 30% in the 1000-500 mb layer)\nand early November insolation will support highs reaching around 60 \nto the lower 60s, aided by 925 mb temperatures warming to near 10 C \nacross southern areas.\n\nThe primary focus for the medium-range period remains a significant \npattern shift slated for the late week, as a full-latitude longwave \ntrough develops over the eastern CONUS. Upper-level energy and \nassociated 6-hour height falls tracking through the Pacific \nNorthwest this evening will translate into the western Ohio Valley \nby late Wednesday, influenced and steered by a high-latitude upper-\nlevel wave/500 mb low progged to dig aggressively southward out of \nthe Quebec/Hudson Bay region Wednesday into Thursday. A sharp cold \nfront tied to the initial height falls will cross SE MI late \nWednesday night into Thursday, abruptly ending the mild stretch. \nBehind the front, deep northwest flow will usher in an unseasonably \ncold arctic-sourced airmass, driving robust CAA that drops 850 mb \ntemperatures to around -8 C by Friday. Periodic pulses of synoptic- \nscale ascent will traverse the region through late week via pockets \nof PVA tied to secondary shortwaves pinwheeling around the primary \nclosed low.\n\nLocally, the most impactful sensible weather will stem from the lake \nresponse. The arrival of the cold pool aloft will steepen low-to-mid-\nlevel lapse rates and foster deep convective mixing. With Lake Huron \nsurface temperatures hovering around 10-12 C, over-water thermal \ndifferentials will reach around 20 C, generating lake-induced CAPE \nof 300-600 J/kg and inversion heights climbing well above 800 mb. \nThis setup supports a prolonged period of lake-effect precipitation \nThursday through Saturday. Thursday looks to feature the most \nwidespread coverage, as brisk northeast low-level flow drives showers\nwell inland. Local probabilistic guidance indicates wind gusts to 30\nmph Thursday, making ambient temperatures in the 40s feel \nconsiderably colder. Given the thermal profile, daytime precipitation\nwill favor a rain/melting snow mix late, transitioning primarily to \nwet/melting snow showers during the overnight and morning hours. As \nlow-level winds back more north-northwest to northwest Friday into \nSaturday, the most organized banding will reorient toward the Thumb \nand eastern tier, though diurnal snow/graupel showers will still \nscatter inland under the core of the cold pool. There is some \npotential for light snow accumulation Thursday night into Friday \nshould wet-bulb zero heights descend to 1,000 ft AGL or below. \nGuidance indicates a sharp thermal boundary, with marine influences \nholding wet-bulb zero heights at or above 2,000 ft along the \nimmediate eastern Thumb shoreline. Probabilistic guidance suggests \npredominantly melting snow on paved surfaces, though chances (<30%) \nfor minor accumulations exist and have been trending slightly upward.\nSpecifics remain difficult to pin down this far out given the \nborderline boundary-layer thermal structure. \n\nHeights slowly rebound over the weekend as sprawling surface high \npressure over the western Great Lakes/Ohio Valley slides eastward. \nLarge-scale subsidence will cap convective depths and shut down lake-\neffect activity, with 850 mb temperatures moderating toward 0 C by \nSunday afternoon. Mostly clear skies and light winds Sunday night \nwill provide ideal radiational cooling conditions, allowing minimum \ntemperatures to drop back into the upper 20s to lower 30s.",
